WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.900
Complete coverage index, part one.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.567
Here's the long tail of vocabulary, one line each, so no term catches you off guard.

3
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:11.777
Models come with type labels.

4
00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:16.353
A foundation model is raw and pretrained, and just completes text.

5
00:00:16.353 --> 00:00:21.900
Instruct models follow instructions, and chat models handle multi-turn dialogue.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.333
Code models specialize in code, and reasoning models are trained to think before answering.

7
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:32.233
Embedding and reranker models output vectors and relevance scores, not text.

8
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:40.838
You'll see concrete releases in job descriptions, like GPT-4o and o3, Claude 3.

9
00:00:40.838 --> 00:00:42.619
5 Sonnet, Gemini 1.

10
00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:46.181
5 Pro, Llama 3 and 4, Mixtral, Qwen 2.

11
00:00:46.181 --> 00:00:47.400
5, and Phi-4.

12
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.533
Version numbers move fast, so learn the families, not the numbers.

13
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:57.933
Layer normalization normalizes activations across features, within each token.

14
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:04.267
That keeps training stable, and modern transformers apply it before each attention and feed-forward block.

15
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:08.500
RMSNorm is the cheaper variant that LLMs now use.

16
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.567
Sparse attention restricts which positions can attend to each other.

17
00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:21.133
Instead of all pairs, it uses a subset, with strided, local, or block patterns, which reduces the quadratic cost.

18
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:24.567
Sliding-window attention is the best-known example.

19
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:30.867
Speech to text is Whisper-class transcription, and text to speech is voice synthesis.

20
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:38.400
Flow-based models are invertible generative models with exact likelihoods, the ancestor of today's flow matching.

21
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.033
Five prompting techniques from the long tail.

22
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:46.800
Tree of Thoughts and Graph of Thoughts branch and evaluate several reasoning paths.

23
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.233
Plan-and-Solve plans first, then executes the plan.

24
00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:54.933
Generated Knowledge has the model state relevant facts before answering.

25
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:59.900
APE uses an LLM to generate and select prompts automatically.

26
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.200
And Directional Stimulus injects hint keywords to steer generation.

27
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.200
These are sampling knobs that reduce repetition.

28
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:16.567
The frequency penalty scales tokens down by how often they've appeared, while the presence penalty penalizes any token that appeared at all.

29
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:19.067
They help on long generations that loop.

30
00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:22.267
Three tools for reliable output.

31
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:27.467
Schema forcing constrains generation so output must match a JSON schema.

32
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:33.733
Output parsing converts the text into typed objects, like Pydantic, with retry on failure.

33
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:39.100
System-prompt hardening writes the prompt defensively, a mitigation, never a boundary.

34
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.200
And the long tail of RAG variants.

35
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:42.953
RAG 2.

36
00:02:42.953 --> 00:02:49.300
0 optimizes retrieval and generation end to end, rather than bolting parts together.

37
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.567
Hybrid RAG combines dense and sparse retrieval.

38
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:56.267
Multi-modal RAG retrieves images and tables too.

39
00:02:56.567 --> 00:03:00.000
Multi-vector RAG keeps several embeddings per document.

40
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.767
Long-context RAG retrieves, then feeds very large context windows.

41
00:03:05.967 --> 00:03:06.676
Let's recap.

42
00:03:06.676 --> 00:03:10.933
Know the model type labels, and learn the families, not version numbers.

43
00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:15.267
Layer norm and sparse attention keep transformers stable and cheaper.

44
00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:19.033
Sampling penalties and schema forcing control the output.

45
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:22.967
And RAG variants are combinations of the same building blocks.
